We need to produce 5 integers:

1. stock_at_target: the stock level at target_time (after processing all selected events up to target_time)
2. historical_lost: total lost quantity through target_time using visible revisions (i.e., sum of unmet demand lost during the historical period)
3. stock_at_horizon: stock at horizon_time after applying the future_schedule on top of the reconstructed stock
4. future_lost: total lost quantity in future scenario (i.e., sum of unmet demand lost in the future schedule)
5. first_future_stockout_time: earliest event_time in future_schedule with unmet demand (or -1 if none). Not counting historical stockouts.

We need to follow the instructions carefully.

The instructions say:

- For raw report histories: first keep reports with received_time <= knowledge_cutoff. So we filter the reports that were received (i.e., known) by the knowledge_cutoff time. In this case, knowledge_cutoff is 100. So we keep reports with received_time <= 100. So any reports with received_time > 100 are ignored (i.e., not visible at the knowledge cutoff). There are many with received_time > 100, so we will discard them.

- Then choose the highest visible revision for EACH event_id. Revisions REPLACE earlier reports, not add to them. So for each event_id, we need to look at all reports with that event_id that have received_time <= 100; pick the report with the maximum revision number. That selected report will be the chosen representation for that event_id. That selected event has event_time, kind, quantity, revision (the maximum). So the earlier reports of that event_id are ignored, replaced.

- Then keep chosen events with event_time <= target_time. So we only consider events whose event_time <= 100. Those are events in the historical period up to target time (100). So we only consider events up to time 100. Events beyond 100 (i.e., event_time > 100) are ignored.

- Apply these events in (event_time, event_id) order, starting from initial_stock. So we sort the selected events first by event_time ascending, then event_id ascending. We then process them in that order. For each event: if receipt, we add quantity to stock. If demand, we consume min(stock, quantity) from stock, and any unmet demand is lost. Unmet demand lost is quantity - consumed. But we do not backorder. So lost = max(0, quantity - stock_before). Then reduce stock by min(stock, quantity). That's typical lost-sales.

- We must ignore any report not visible at the knowledge cutoff, even if its event occurred earlier. So we only use the visible selected events.

- "Arrival order is not event order." This is just a note that the order of reports (i.e., received_time) is not necessarily the order of events; we handle by event_time, event_id order.

